FAQ: Pusat Pemulihan Penagihan Narkotik (PUSPEN)
Q1: What is PUSPEN?
A: PUSPEN is a drug rehabilitation centre that offers treatment and rehabilitation to individuals certified as drug dependents by a Registered Medical Officer. Admission can be voluntary under Section 8(3)(a) or by court order under Section 6(1)(a) of the Drug Addicts (R&P) Act 1983. It also accepts adolescents under Section 9 of the same act.

Q2: How can someone voluntarily get treatment at PUSPEN?
A: Individuals may walk in to any PUSPEN or visit a nearby District AADK Office for information and to initiate the voluntary admission process.

Q3: Who is eligible for PUSPEN services?
A: -Drug addicts
   -Drug users
   -Drug abusers
   -Co-dependents (family members involved in drug-related issues)

Q4: Are there any admission fees at PUSPEN?
A: No, there are no fees for admission.

Q5: What types of treatment and rehabilitation programs are provided at PUSPEN?
A: Programs are personalized and include:
	- Detoxification
	- Physical activities and discipline
	- Psychological support
	- Patriotism
	- Skills and vocational training
	- Religion and spirituality
	- Social and recreational activities
	- Community integration




FAQ: Community House
Q1: What services are offered at the Community House?
A:	- Guidance programmes
	- Religious and spiritual programmes
	- Advisory services related to drugs
	- Treatment, rehabilitation, and drug prevention activities

Q2: Who runs the activities at the Community House?
A: Activities are organized by:
	- Community House committee members
	- District NADA officers
	- Peer counsellors
	- Local community members

Q3: Who can I contact to contribute ideas or experiences to the Community House?
A: Contact the Chief of the District NADA supervising the relevant Community House.

Q4: Can I become part of the Community House Committee?
A: Yes, if you are a resident of the area served by the Community House.

Q5: Who can access the Community House services?
A:	- Individuals with drug issues
	- Local community members
	- The public
	- Students
	- Employers and employees
	- Families




FAQ: NADA Service Centre
Q1: What is the NADA Service Centre?
A: It is a daily activity centre offering support for individuals with drug-related issues.

Q2: What is the purpose of the NADA Service Centre?
A:	- Provide treatment and rehabilitation
	- Offer referral and advisory services
	- Deliver caring-based services
	- Conduct prevention, counseling, and intervention programs

Q3: What is a Psychosocial Program?
A: A treatment program to:	- Change addictive behaviors
							- Build relapse prevention skills
							- Provide recovery knowledge
							- Help clients function normally in society




FAQ: Client Integration Centre (CIC)
Q1: What is CIC?
A: CIC is a rehabilitation service facility for clients who have completed PUSPEN programs, focusing on reintegration into the community.

Q2: What is the purpose of CIC?
A:	- Empower clients’ recovery
	- Strengthen self-efficacy
	- Provide skills training and job opportunities

Q3: What is meant by residency in CIC?
A: It refers to clients living in CIC for 3 to 6 months as part of an aftercare program.

Q4: What is the psychosocial program in CIC?
A: Similar to other centres, it includes behavior change, relapse prevention, and confidence-building to function in society.




FAQ: Enforcement and Security
Q1: Will my identity be kept confidential if I report someone?
A: Yes, your information is confidential and not shared with others.

Q2: Will the drug addict know I reported them?
A: No, AADK ensures the reporter’s identity remains confidential.

Q3: Can my son be admitted directly to PUSPEN after a report?
A: Admission requires a court order, following drug testing and medical confirmation. This process may take 2 weeks or more.

Q4: Why was a family member released after 14 days of remand?
A: The law limits detention to 14 days while awaiting lab confirmation. If results aren't ready, the person is released on bail.

Q5: Can a person under AADK supervision be sent to PUSPEN again?
A: No. If they violate supervision terms, AADK will investigate, and the court may impose penalties under Section 6(3).

Q6: Is it safe for me to report drug abuse?
A: Yes, your safety and data are protected. Reports are kept confidential.

Q7: Why is my son still using drugs after rehabilitation?
A: Addiction is a chronic disease with risk of relapse. Continued supervision, treatment, and family support are essential for recovery.

Q8: Why are drug-positive individuals granted bail?
A: Bail may be granted if the individual has chronic health issues or if lab results are delayed beyond the 14-day remand period.

Q9: Why are addicts released instead of being sent straight to PUSPEN?
A: Legal procedures require drug confirmation and court orders. AADK cannot detain individuals beyond 14 days without lab results.

Q10: Why doesn’t AADK arrest drug dealers instead?
A: AADK focuses on addicts (demand reduction). PDRM handles drug trafficking cases (supply reduction).

Q11: How long will it take to get feedback after lodging a complaint?
A: Within 3 working days.

Q12: Will I be informed of any action taken?
A: Yes, you will be notified of the actions taken.




FAQ: Antidrugs SKUAD
Q1: What is the Antidrugs SKUAD?
A: A volunteer arm of NADA that conducts anti-drug activities within communities.

Q2: What are the roles of Antidrugs SKUAD members?
A:	- Raise awareness about drug dangers
	- Support those with drug problems
	- Promote NADA services

Q3: How do I join the Antidrugs SKUAD?
A: Malaysian citizens aged 18+ can apply online at skuad.adk.gov.my or via the mobile app (Google Play Store / iOS App Store).




FAQ: Private Drug Rehabilitation Centre (PPDP)
Q1: What is a private drug rehabilitation centre?
A: A centre registered under Section 16 of the Drug Dependants Act 1983 to provide treatment programs either on-site or externally.

Q2: How to apply for registration (gazetting) of a PPDP?
A: Submit an official application to the State NADA Director, meeting all required conditions.

Q3: How long is the PPDP registration certificate valid?
A: As long as the centre operates in compliance with stipulated conditions.

Q4: When can a PPDP certificate be revoked?
A: If the centre fails to comply with the terms and conditions of approval.




FAQ: NADA Research Grant
Application for Academic Research/Study/Writing
Q1: Can academic research be conducted at NADA Drug Rehabilitation Centres?
A: Only at Cure & Care Clinics, with approval from NADA Headquarters.

Q2: What is the procedure to obtain approval for academic research?
A: Submit:	- Application letter
			- University confirmation letter
			- Research proposal with questionnaire
			- Submit at least 1 month in advance
			- If approved, sign an agreement with NADA
			- If no response in 2 months, it is considered rejected
			- Submit a hardbound copy of research findings to NADA HQ

Research under the NADA Research Grant
Q3: Does NADA provide research grants?
A: Yes, for studies on drugs/substance abuse and developing new or improved drug-related services or programs.

Q4: Who can apply?
A: Researchers from local Higher Education Institutions.

Q5: What is the procedure to apply for a NADA Research Grant?
A: Submit a proposal using the NADA Research Grant Form (downloadable from NADA website)
Send completed applications to:
Director General, National Anti-Drugs Agency, Ministry of Home Affairs
Attn: Director of Policy, Planning and Research Division
Tel: 03-8911 2200 | Fax: 03-8925 9306

The Steering Committee will evaluate the application
